.TH smi-upload "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-upload \- Upload a file to a remote host over SSH using scp
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-upload
.I SOURCE_FILE
.I DESTINATION_FILE
.RI [[ USER @] HOST [: PORT ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-upload
copies the local
.I SOURCE_FILE
to
.I DESTINATION_FILE
on a remote host using
.BR scp (1).
.PP
Arguments are positional: the local file first, the remote path second.
Host name, port, user name and password are taken from environment variables,
so that credentials stay out of the command line and out of the shell history.
The optional third argument overrides user, host and port for one call.
The variables are the same ones
.BR smi-remote-exec (1)
reads, so an upload and the remote commands that follow it share one
configuration.
.PP
When a password is set, it is handed to
.BR sshpass (1)
through the
.SM SSHPASS
environment variable, never as a command line argument, so it does not appear
in the process list.
When no password is set,
.B scp
runs on its own and key based authentication (or an interactive password
prompt) is used;
.B sshpass
is not needed in that case.
.PP
An unknown host key is accepted and stored without asking
.RB ( StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new ),
so the command works unattended.
A host key that has changed since it was stored is still refused.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I SOURCE_FILE
Local file to upload.
Must exist and be a regular file.
.TP
.I DESTINATION_FILE
Path on the remote host.
A relative path is resolved against the remote user's home directory.
The remote parent directory must already exist.
.TP
.RI [ USER @] HOST [: PORT ]
Remote server for this invocation only.
Overrides
.SM SMI_REMOTE_HOST
and, when present, the
.I USER
part overrides
.SM SMI_REMOTE_USER
and the
.I PORT
part overrides
.SM SMI_REMOTE_PORT\c
\&.
Example:
.BR deploy@files.example.com:2222 .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SMI_REMOTE_HOST
Remote host name or IP address.
No default; required unless the host is given as the third argument.
.TP
.B SMI_REMOTE_PORT
Remote SSH port.
Default:
.BR 22 .
.TP
.B SMI_REMOTE_USER
Remote user name.
Default: current OS login name.
.TP
.B SMI_REMOTE_PASSWORD
Remote user's password.
Default: empty, which means key based authentication without
.BR sshpass (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file was uploaded.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong usage, no host given, the source file is missing, or a password is set but
.B sshpass
is not installed.
.TP
.I other
Exit status of
.BR scp (1)
or
.BR sshpass (1);
.B sshpass
returns
.B 5
for a wrong password and
.B 6
for an unknown host key.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Upload with key based authentication, server as the third argument:
.RS
.nf
smi-upload build/app.war /srv/incoming/app.war deploy@files.example.com:2222
.fi
.RE
.PP
Upload with a password, everything from the environment:
.RS
.nf
SMI_REMOTE_HOST=files.example.com SMI_REMOTE_PORT=2222 \\
SMI_REMOTE_USER=deploy SMI_REMOTE_PASSWORD=secret \\
smi-upload build/app.war /srv/incoming/app.war
.fi
.RE
.PP
Upload a deployment archive, then move it into place on the same host:
.RS
.nf
export SMI_REMOTE_HOST=files.example.com SMI_REMOTE_USER=deploy
smi-upload build/a.tar.gz deployments/a.tar.gz
smi-remote-exec mv '~/deployments/a.tar.gz' /var/opt/a/deploy/a.tar.gz
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep the password in the environment, choose the server per call:
.RS
.nf
export SMI_REMOTE_PASSWORD=secret
smi-upload report.pdf reports/report.pdf alice@intranet.example.com
.fi
.RE
.SH AUTHOR
.PP
Command written by Imre Tabur.
